## Outcome

Determine whether the active semantic policy leaves a material gap where locally created mutable identity escapes an ownership-known boundary, then define the smallest ownership-aware enforcement needed if the gap is real.

This issue decides and prototypes the guard. It does not assume that a blanket mutation ban or a new rule is required.

## Escape cases to evaluate

* returning a mutable object or collection;
* returning an iterator, element, or closure that exposes captured mutable state;
* storing local mutable identity into caller-reachable state;
* yielding mutable identity from a generator;
* passing mutable identity to an unresolved callee that can retain it;
* shallow-freezing an object that still exposes nested mutable identities;
* exporting a frozen wrapper whose methods expose mutable handles.

## Accepted controls to evaluate

* a local array or `Map` converted to a deeply readonly result;
* a parser cursor returning primitives or a fresh frozen tree;
* a bounded state machine returning a branded or deeply readonly value;
* a closure-private cache whose API exposes no keys, values, iterators, or mutable handles.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Measure current coverage for every escape case and accepted control.
- [ ] Give each accepted control a negative twin that differs only by an escaping identity.
- [ ] Separate retained identity from unresolved opaque effects; do not invent opacity to represent retention.
- [ ] Decide whether the gap belongs in the existing semantic analyzer, another project rule, type design, or documentation only.
- [ ] Specify ownership provenance, cross-file summary, caching, and diagnostic requirements for any selected analyzer change.
- [ ] Reject a blanket mutation ban unless evidence proves ownership-aware enforcement cannot express the required boundary.
- [ ] Create an implementation follow-up only if the decision selects new enforcement.

## Scope boundaries

* `AQU-410` owns enabling the three extracted effect rules.
* `AQU-414` and `AQU-438` own known analyzer correctness and observer-retention defects.
* `AQU-444` defines the ownership policy this decision must implement.

